\begin{align*}
x_1 + x_2 + x_3 &= 6 \\
x_1x_2 + x_1x_3 + x_2x_3 &= 21 \\
x_1x_2x_3 &= -a
\end{align*}
Substituting $x_2$ and $x_3$ into these equations, we get
\begin{align*}
3x_1 + 3d &= 6 \\
3x_1^2 + 6x_1d + 2d^2 &= 21 \\
x_1(x_1 + d)(x_1 + 2d) &= -a
\end{align*}
From the first equation, we have $x_1 + d = 2$, so $x_2 = 2$. Substituting this into the second equation, we get
\begin{align*}
3x_1^2 + 12x_1 + 12 &= 21 \\
3x_1^2 + 12x_1 - 9 &= 0 \\
x_1^2 + 4x_1 - 3 &= 0 \\
(x_1 + 2)^2 &= 7 \\
x_1 &= -2 \pm \sqrt{7}
\end{align*}
